7. Installed Version Verification Process

Before initiating any remediation activity, the EUC (Endpoint Engineering) team performs a detailed validation and impact assessment process to confirm the vulnerable application versions, deployment feasibility, and remediation approach.

The purpose of this process is to:

* Validate the vulnerable application footprint across the environment
* Identify affected device population
* Verify whether updated secure versions are already deployed
* Assess deployment readiness
* Determine remediation approach using Intune and Patch My PC
* Minimize business and operational impact during remediation

⸻

7.1 Workorder Review & Initial Analysis

Once the workorder/ticket is assigned by the Risk and Compliance team, the EUC team begins the remediation analysis process.

The assigned engineer reviews:

* Vulnerability details
* CVE reference
* Affected application
* Installed vulnerable version
* Recommended fixed version
* Affected device count
* Severity level
* Remediation SLA
* Vendor recommendations

The engineer also validates whether:

* The vulnerability is application-related
* The vulnerability is OS patch-related
* The remediation already exists in the environment
* The vulnerability is linked to an ongoing patch cycle

⸻

7.2 Installed Version Validation Process

The EUC team validates the currently installed application versions across managed devices to determine:

* Existing vulnerable versions
* Deployment coverage
* Devices pending remediation
* Devices already updated
* Non-reporting or inactive devices

⸻

7.3 Validation Using Microsoft Intune

The primary validation platform used is:
Microsoft Intune

Intune Validation Steps

Step 1 – Login to Intune Admin Center

Navigate to:

* Microsoft Intune Admin Center
* Apps
* Monitor
* Discovered Apps

⸻

Step 2 – Search Vulnerable Application

Search for:

* Vulnerable application name

Example:

* Google Chrome
* Adobe Acrobat
* Zoom
* Notepad++

The EUC team validates:

* Installed application versions
* Number of affected devices
* Existing deployment status
* Latest deployed version

⸻

Step 3 – Validate Existing Deployment

The team verifies whether:

* The latest secure version is already deployed
* Supersedence is configured
* Deployment assignment exists
* Devices are checking into Intune properly

The following checks are performed:

* Install status
* Detection status
* Assignment status
* Deployment failures
* Device compliance status

⸻

7.4 Vulnerable Application Footprint Analysis

The EUC team analyzes the application footprint to determine:

* Total affected device population
* Business-critical device impact
* Regional impact
* Department-specific impact
* VIP or executive device impact
* Cloud PC impact
* Shared device impact

This analysis helps define:

* Deployment priority
* Pilot group selection
* Deployment ring strategy
* Communication requirements

⸻

7.5 Patch My PC Version Verification

For third-party applications, the EUC team validates the latest secure version availability in:
Patch My PC

⸻

Validation Activities

The engineer performs the following checks:

Application Availability

Verify whether the application exists in Patch My PC catalog.

⸻

Latest Version Validation

Validate:

* Latest available version
* Release date
* Vendor release notes
* Security fixes included
* Supported architecture (x64/x86)

⸻

Deployment Readiness Validation

Validate:

* Silent install support
* Detection method availability
* Supersedence configuration
* Auto-update capability
* Intune publishing support

⸻

7.6 Windows Update Vulnerability Validation Process

If the vulnerability is related to:

* Windows cumulative updates
* Security updates
* Feature updates
* Microsoft patches

then the EUC team validates the vulnerability against the current patch cycle.

⸻

7.7 Monthly Patch Cycle Validation

The engineering team checks whether the vulnerability belongs to:

* Current month patch cycle
* Previous month patch cycle (N-1)
* Out-of-band security update
* Emergency patch release

⸻

Current Month Patch Validation

If the vulnerability belongs to the current patch cycle:

* The EUC team validates deployment progress in Intune
* Update compliance is checked
* Devices pending installation are identified
* Risk and Compliance team is informed

In such scenarios, the Risk and Compliance team may temporarily exclude the vulnerability until the patch deployment cycle completes.

⸻

N-1 Patch Compliance Validation

If the vulnerability does not belong to the current month cycle, the EUC team checks:

* N-1 update compliance status
* Devices missing previous month updates
* Deployment failures
* Safeguard holds
* Device update errors

Validation is performed through:

* Intune Update Compliance Reports
* Windows Update for Business reports
* Device update ring status
* Feature update reports

⸻

7.8 Business Impact Assessment

Before remediation deployment, the EUC team performs business impact analysis to identify:

* Critical business applications
* Potential downtime risks
* Restart requirements
* Application dependency conflicts
* User productivity impact
* VPN or authentication impact

The assessment helps determine:

* Deployment scheduling
* Maintenance windows
* Pilot device selection
* Communication requirements

⸻

7.9 Remediation Feasibility Assessment

The EUC team validates whether:

* The remediation can be deployed silently
* User interaction is required
* Restart is required
* Existing application assignments may conflict
* Deployment can be completed within SLA

The team also validates rollback feasibility by checking:

* Previous application versions
* Uninstall capability
* Restore process
* Deployment rollback options
